Found by the B052/B053 lane, 2026-08-30, while probing ALL 2989 bare value
names of named enums against the live game (Trackmania + Openplanet 1.29.5).
Deliberately kept OUT of B053, which is scoped to the 22 ambiguous names.

## Defect
`has_global_ident`'s bare-value scan walks `source_enums_iter` — EVERY named
enum — instead of only the globally visible ones. So it accepts the values of
roughly 570 nested and namespaced enums whose values are not reachable bare at
all. Measured: **2373 bare names the LSP accepts and the game rejects with
`No matching symbol`.**

False negatives: the LSP stays silent on code that will not compile. This is
100x the size of B053 and is the same defect family.

B044 fixed exactly this shape for ANONYMOUS enums. This is the
named-but-not-globally-visible remainder that B044 did not cover.

## The visibility rule (probe-established, predicts 2987 of 2989)
- An enum is globally visible iff its index key is **depth <= 1 with the
  synthetic module prefix stripped** — 68 keys, **60 distinct names**.
  Nested enums never are; Core namespaced enums never are.
- A bare value resolves iff exactly ONE such enum carries it; >= 2 is the
  ambiguity error (B053); 0 is `No matching symbol` (this ticket).

The 2 residuals are `Main` and `RGBAColor`, which hit an unrelated symbol in
the probe rather than being counterexamples.

**Dedup by bare name is required.** The loader keys ONE engine enum under
several modules — `Hms::EHmsLightMapQuality` and
`MetaNotPersistent::EHmsLightMapQuality` are the same enum. Counting keys
instead of names is what inflated B053's original estimate by 34x.

## Why this is separable from B053, and what it inherits
B053 must teach the index which enums are globally visible in order to
diagnose its 22 ambiguous names. That is the SAME machinery this ticket needs,
so whoever takes this inherits a working visibility model rather than starting
cold. Land B053 first.

## Work
- Narrow the bare-value scan to globally-visible enums only.
- Re-verify against the game after implementing rather than trusting the
  model to generalise beyond the probed corpus.
- Reuse the B052/B053 lane's probe corpus and per-name verdict table; it must
  be committed in-repo (see B056). Do not re-probe 2989 names from scratch.

## Probe hygiene (from B056, applies here)
Other installed plugins' namespaces leak into the compile, so a game REJECT is
solid evidence about the engine while a game ACCEPT on an unknown prefix is
not. Apply that asymmetry or you will encode this machine's plugin set as
engine behaviour.

## Gate
This will RAISE fixture ERROR counts substantially — that is the fix working,
and it is why this is separate from B053. Every new error must be justified by
name against a recorded game REJECT. An unexplained new error is a bug, not a
baseline update. Measure two binaries on the same machine at the same time,
and re-measure baselines first: B052 and B053 land before this.

